@main(class="manual", id="guide")
{
	@aside(class="guide-sidebar")
	{
		@p(class="toc-label") {Guide}
		@toc(start=1)
	}

	@article(class="article")
	{
		@h1(id="what") {What}

		@p {
			Manny is an all-in-one, radically minimal build system for C and C++ projects.
		}

		@p {
			The core interface is one single function: @code{manny.build(...)}. It
			takes a plain list of target tasks and handles the rest.
		}

		@p {
			What makes Manny powerful is that you can use the script to generate tasks whichever way you want.
		}

		@p {
			Manny’s core remains simple while the script adapts to your project’s needs.
		}

		@p {
			There are three core layers:
		}

		@ul {
			@li {A general-purpose parallel graph executor.}
			@li {A tight build system built on top.}
			@li {The build system exposed to a scripting language through one function.}
		}
		@p {
			For the script, we use @code{elf}: a custom, minimal, general-purpose
			scripting language. Its main data structure is the table.
		}

		@h1(id="why") {Why}
		@p {I despise build systems, especially the “good” ones.}
		@p {They usually fall into one of three camps:}
		@ol(type=1) {
			@li {An ugly, inconsistent DSL with no real power.}
			@li {An enormous machine nobody completely understands.}
			@li {A script that gives you freedom by making you implement the entire build system yourself.}
		}
		@p {And no, I did not want to write my C builds in Python.}
		@p {I wanted a simple tool that would allow me to build my C projects.}
		@p {
			Coming from Handmade Hero, I was used to the unity build approach and a
			batch file. But sometimes you actually need multiple compilation units.
		}
		@p {The problem is that existing systems are massive, cumbersome, and just plain annoying.}
		@p {Ninja forces me to still rely on some other program and a weird DSL.}
		@p {CMake is massive and has an unfamiliar DSL.}
		@p {Using something like Make doesn’t cut it either because it has the same DSL problem.}
		@p {
			Meson forces me to use its project descriptors and makes custom,
			general-purpose work awkward or impossible.
		}
		@p {Virtually everything else forces me to have Python or some other thing.}
		@p {Why can’t I have a single, tiny executable to build my C project?}
		@p {
			A build script should be a real program. It should have functions, loops,
			tables, modules, and normal control flow. It should be able to inspect
			arguments, generate files, load another project, or do something completely
			unreasonable because the project requires it.
		}
		@p {
			But the script should not have to schedule work, track headers, propagate
			failures, maintain incremental state, or figure out what changed.
		}
		@p {@strong{That is Manny’s job.}}
		@p {
			Manny is a self-contained, general-purpose, programmable build system with
			a bounded, well-defined core. Unlike build-system generators such as Meson
			and CMake, Manny does not generate files for another tool to execute. It
			constructs and runs the task graph itself.
		}
		@p {
			Manny has no executable target type. It has no static-library target type.
			It does not need to understand your directory layout, decide what your
			project should look like, or generate files for another build system to execute.
		}
		@p {It receives the work and builds it.}
		@p {
			Manny handles parallel execution, compiler-discovered dependencies,
			fingerprints, persistent state, interruption, failure propagation, and
			explaining why something rebuilt. The script remains free to generate the
			graph however it wants.
		}
		@p {
			I can write my own project descriptors, scan folders, and build everything
			within them. I can use regular variables, control flow, and such. I can do
			arbitrary work and call arbitrary stuff. I can literally do anything because
			we’re not shipping a restrictive model, we’re shipping a minimal library.
		}
		@p {Generate the table however you want. Give it to Manny.}
		@p {That’s freaking it.}
		@p {Manny is still in its early stages, but thus far, it shows great promise.}

		@h1(id="how") {How}
		@p {
			A @code{build.elf} file is an elf program. It creates tasks as plain tables,
			connects them through direct references, and passes the targets you want to
			build to @code{manny.build()}.
		}
		@code(language="elf")
		{{
			elf.fs.create_directories("build")

			compile := {
				name = "compile main",
				command_line = "clang-cl /c main.c /Fobuild/main.obj",
				inputs = { "main.c" },
				outputs = { "build/main.obj" },
			}

			link := {
				name = "link hello",
				command_line = "clang-cl build/main.obj /Febuild/hello.exe",
				inputs = { "build/main.obj" },
				outputs = { "build/hello.exe" },
				dependencies = { compile },
			}

			entries := {}

			entries.build = fun() {
				ret manny.build({ targets = { link } })
			}

			ret entries
		}}
		@p {A task is a table. Dependencies are references to other tasks. The selected tasks become a graph. Manny executes that graph.}
		@p {Now, defining an entire project exactly like this would be fairly laborious. This is almost like writing Ninja directly.}
		@p {So instead, you can use the script itself to make it easier.}
		@p {
			You can create your own project descriptor as plain tables, translate those
			to tasks, and build your project. Use functions, loops, modules, filesystem
			inspection, or whatever else the project needs. In the end, Manny only needs
			the task graph.
		}
		@p {The step-by-step guide starts with running @code{build.elf} and builds up from there.}

		@h1(id="step-by-step-guide") {Guide}
		@p {This guide starts with an ordinary elf script and builds up to an incremental task graph one piece at a time.}

		@h2(id="getting-manny") {Get Manny}
		@p {Manny currently supports Windows x64.}
		@p {
			Download and extract the latest
			@a(href="https://github.com/MicroRJ/Manny/releases/tag/v0.3.0-dev") {Windows preview}.
			Add the directory containing @code{manny.exe} to your @code{PATH}, or invoke
			it using its full path.
		}
		@p {Check that it runs:}
		@code(language="bat") {{
			manny --version
		}}
		@p {To build Manny from source instead, clone the repository with its submodules and run @code{build.bat}:}
		@code(language="bat")
		{{
			git clone --recursive https://github.com/MicroRJ/Manny.git
			cd Manny
			build.bat
		}}
		@p {The new executable is written to @code{build\manny.exe}.}

		@h2(id="running-buildelf") {Run build.elf}
		@p {Create an empty directory and add a file named @code{build.elf}:}
		@code(language="elf")
		{{
			elf.printl("Hello, World!")

			ret {
				build = fun() {},
			}
		}}
		@p {Now run Manny from that directory:}
		@code(language="bat") {{
			manny
		}}
		@p {
			Manny looks for @code{build.elf}, loads it, and invokes the @code{build}
			entry by default. The first line runs while the script is loaded. It is
			ordinary elf code, not a special build-system declaration.
		}

		@h2(id="entries") {Add entries}
		@p {The table returned by @code{build.elf} defines the entries you can invoke. Replace the script with this:}
		@code(language="elf")
		{{
			ret {
				build = fun() {
					elf.printl("Building")
				},

				hello = fun() {
					elf.printl("Hello")
				},
			}
		}}
		@p {Running Manny without an entry still invokes @code{build}:}
		@code(language="bat") {{
			manny
		}}
		@p {Pass another entry by name to invoke it instead:}
		@code(language="bat") {{
			manny hello
		}}
		@p {Entries are normal elf functions. They can inspect data, call other functions, generate files, or do whatever else your project needs.}

		@h2(id="tasks") {Build a task}
		@p {Now replace the script with one that gives Manny an actual task:}
		@code(language="elf")
		{{
			elf.fs.create_directories("build")

			write_message := {
				name = "write message",
				command_line = "cmd /d /c echo Hello from Manny>build/message.txt",
				inputs = {},
				outputs = { "build/message.txt" },
				dependencies = {},
			}

			ret {
				build = fun() {
					ret manny.build({ targets = { write_message } })
				},
			}
		}}
		@p {Run it:}
		@code(language="bat") {{
			manny
		}}
		@p {
			The entry creates no special target object. @code{write_message} is a plain
			table, and @code{manny.build()} receives it as the target to build.
		}

		@h2(id="dependencies") {Add a dependency}
		@p {Tasks depend directly on other task tables:}
		@code(language="elf")
		{{
			elf.fs.create_directories("build")

			write_source := {
				name = "write source message",
				command_line = "cmd /d /c echo Hello from Manny>build/source.txt",
				inputs = {},
				outputs = { "build/source.txt" },
				dependencies = {},
			}

			copy_message := {
				name = "copy message",
				command_line = "cmd /d /c type build\source.txt>build\message.txt",
				inputs = { "build/source.txt" },
				outputs = { "build/message.txt" },
				dependencies = { write_source },
			}

			ret {
				build = fun() {
					ret manny.build({ targets = { copy_message } })
				},
			}
		}}
		@p {
			Manny starts from @code{copy_message}, follows the reference to
			@code{write_source}, and constructs the graph. Dependencies run before the
			tasks that need them.
		}

		@h2(id="incremental-builds") {See incremental builds}
		@p {Run the build again without changing anything:}
		@code(language="bat") {{
			manny
		}}
		@p {Both tasks remain current because their commands, inputs, outputs, and dependencies have not changed.}
		@p {Change the message in @code{write_source.command_line}, then ask Manny to explain the next build:}
		@code(language="bat") {{
			manny --explain
		}}
		@p {
			Manny rebuilds @code{write_source} because its command changed, then rebuilds
			@code{copy_message} because its input changed.
		}
		@p {
			That is the complete model: elf generates task tables, dependencies connect
			them, and @code{manny.build()} builds the selected graph.
		}
	}
}
